{
  "result": "There's a specific kind of quiet that only shows up at 3 a.m. It's not silence, not really. It's a thick, soft quiet that lets you hear things you normally miss. The refrigerator humming in the kitchen. The faint tick of the radiator cooling. The house settling, like it's exhaling after holding its breath all day.\n\nI used to be afraid of that quiet when I was a kid. Every creak was a monster, every shadow a question mark. Now I find it comforting. It reminds me that even inanimate things have a life cycle, a rhythm. Houses breathe. Wood expands and contracts. Pipes groan as water moves through them like blood.\n\nWe live so much of our lives with headphones in, podcasts playing, notifications buzzing, that we forget how to just exist in a room without filling it. But in that late-night quiet, you're not filling anything. You're listening.\n\nI keep thinking about how much of the world exists when we're not paying attention. The street outside my window doesn't stop when I close the blinds. The oak tree across the road keeps growing, inch by slow inch, whether I notice or not. There's something humbling and also freeing about that.\n\nMaybe that's why I like staying up a little too late sometimes. Not because I'm productive, not because I'm avoiding tomorrow, but because for a few minutes, I get to hear the world as it is when it's not performing for anyone. And that feels like a secret gift.",
